<h2>The case in one view</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Case</th><td>J.P. v. State (Govt. of NCT of Delhi), CRL.A. 502/2025</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Court</th><td>High Court of Delhi, Madhu Jain J</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Decided</th><td>2 November 2026 (reserved 12 October 2026)</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Provisions</th><td>Section 6 r/w Sections 3(b) and 5(m), POCSO Act; Section 29 presumption; appeal under Section 415(2) BNSS</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Outcome</th><td>Appeal dismissed; 20 years&rsquo; RI and fine affirmed</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The background</h2>
<p>The victim was born in October 2018; the incident occurred in November 2021, when she was about three. Her mother, a domestic help, returned home to find the child crying and bleeding; the child said her father had inserted his finger into her private part. The father allegedly threatened the mother and left Delhi for Azamgarh, Uttar Pradesh. The matter reached the police through a PCR call in June 2022 — about seven months later — and he was arrested in Azamgarh days after. The trial court convicted him under Section 6 POCSO in December 2024 and sentenced him to twenty years&rsquo; rigorous imprisonment with fine.</p>

<h2>The child witness, and the tutoring theory</h2>
<p>Age was proved through the birth certificate. On competence and reliability, the court applied the settled standards from <em>Dattu Ramrao Sakhare</em> and <em>Rai Sandeep</em>: a child witness is competent if able to understand and rationally answer questions, and the testimony must be assessed for consistency on the core allegation. The defence theory of tutoring by the mother was rejected on a telling detail — the child&rsquo;s own spontaneous references to matters awkward for the prosecution narrative indicated an unscripted account rather than a coached one.</p>

<h2>Medical neutrality and the delayed report</h2>
<p>The medical examination, conducted months after the incident, found the hymen intact and no injuries. Following <em>State of Punjab v. Gurmit Singh</em>, the court held this was not exculpatory: medical evidence months later is neutral, and corroboration is not an essential prerequisite where the victim&rsquo;s testimony inspires confidence. The seven-month delay in reporting was explained by the child&rsquo;s age, the intra-family character of the offence and fear — reasoning that tracks <em>Tulshidas Kanolkar</em> on the realities of reporting within families.</p>

<h2>The paralysis defence and Section 29</h2>
<p>The appellant&rsquo;s substantive defence was physical impossibility: he suffered from partial paralysis. The court accepted the condition but not the conclusion — the medical opinion found nothing preventing the act, and the evidence showed one-handed functionality. With the foundational facts established, the Section 29 presumption operated, and the defence failed to rebut it. As the trial court had found, and the High Court endorsed, the accused offered no plausible explanation for his claim of false implication.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the record:</strong> the medical witness &ldquo;did not rule out the occurrence of the alleged act&rdquo; &mdash; the finding on which the impossibility defence foundered.</p>
</div>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For the defence.</strong> In POCSO appeals, attacking the child&rsquo;s competence in the abstract rarely succeeds. The productive ground, where it exists, is material inconsistency on the core act — not peripheral discrepancies, which courts routinely discount for very young witnesses.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>On medical evidence.</strong> Where examination is delayed, neutral findings neither convict nor acquit. Counsel on both sides should fix the examination timeline early, because it controls what the medical report can and cannot prove.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>On Section 29.</strong> The presumption is not a formality. Once the prosecution&rsquo;s foundational facts stand, the defence must lead a real rebuttal case — an impossibility plea needs medical proof of impossibility, not merely of impairment.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>Intra-family POCSO cases compress every difficulty of child-sexual-assault prosecution into one file: the youngest witnesses, the longest reporting delays, the most neutral medical records, and defences that trade on both. The Delhi High Court&rsquo;s treatment here — realistic about delay, exacting about the core testimony, and firm on the statutory presumption — is consistent with the appellate course the court has followed across recent POCSO appeals, and it offers a clear map of how such cases are actually won and lost.</p>
